%N A401196 Triangle read by rows: coefficients of the integer numerator polynomials in the expansion of (arcsin(x)/x)^a.
%C A401196 Let c_n(a) = [x^(2*n)](arcsin(x)/x)^a, and let D_n be the positive least common denominator of the rational coefficients of c_n(a). Set P_n(a) = D_n*c_n(a).
%C A401196 Row n lists the coefficients of a^k in P_n(a), in increasing order k = 1, ..., n; the zero constant coefficient is omitted.
%C A401196 D_n = abs(A262179(n+1)). For n >= 1, Lagrange inversion gives c_n(a) = a/(a+2*n)*[t^(2*n)](t/sin(t))^(a+2*n).
%C A401196 Writing [t^(2*n)](t/sin(t))^s = s*h_n(s) gives c_n(a) = a*h_n(a+2*n). Integer translation preserves common denominators, as does replacing t by i*t, proving the relation with A262179.
%H A401196 Eric W. Weisstein, <a href="/A401196/b401196.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..210</a>
%H A401196 Feng Qi, <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/sym16091145">Power Series Expansions of Real Powers of Inverse Cosine and Sine Functions, Closed-Form Formulas of Partial Bell Polynomials at Specific Arguments, and Series Representations of Real Powers of Circular Constant</a>, Symmetry 16 (2024), Art. 1145. See Theorem 7 for the general power-series expansion.
%H A401196 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/InverseSine.html">Inverse Sine</a>.
%F A401196 T(n,k) = [a^k]P_n(a), n >= 1, 1 <= k <= n.
%F A401196 c_0(a) = 1; c_n(a) = (1/n)*Sum_{j=1..n} (((a+1)*j-n)*binomial(2*j,j)/(4^j*(2*j+1)))*c_(n-j)(a).
%e A401196 P_1(a) = a; P_2(a) = 22*a + 5*a^2; P_3(a) = 1528*a + 462*a^2 + 35*a^3.
%e A401196 Triangle begins:
%e A401196 1;
%e A401196 22, 5;
%e A401196 1528, 462, 35;
